  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Damped Sinusoids",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}$ with $\\sigma<0$ as a damped sinusoid by separating the decaying envelope $e^{\\sigma t}$ from the oscillatory factor $e^{j\\omega t}$ and identifying the resulting amplitude decay rate and angular frequency.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Damped Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Damped Sinusoids",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A damped sinusoid is a generalized exponential of the form $e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}$ with $\\sigma<0$. Read the real part $\\sigma$ as the decay part and the imaginary part $\\omega$ as the oscillation part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}=e^{\\sigma t}e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $e^{\\sigma t}$ is the decaying envelope, and the factor $e^{j\\omega t}$ is the oscillatory part. That split is the key move: one factor controls amplitude, and the other factor controls oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left|e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\right|=e^{\\sigma t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\sigma<0$, the envelope gets smaller as $t$ increases, so the amplitude decays at rate $-\\sigma$. The more negative $\\sigma$ is, the faster that decay happens.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sigma<0 \\implies e^{\\sigma t}\\to 0 \\text{ as } t\\to\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The oscillatory factor is still $e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)$, so the angular frequency is $\\omega$ rad/s. In short, $e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}$ is a decaying oscillation whose amplitude falls like $e^{\\sigma t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Damped Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Separating a Damped Complex Exponential into Envelope and Oscillation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Work through $x(t)=e^{(-1.5+j4)t}$ by separating the envelope and oscillatory factor, then label the decay rate and angular frequency.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is the complex exponent $s=-1.5+j4$, so split the signal into its real and imaginary parts. The real part will give the decaying envelope, and the imaginary part will give the oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(-1.5+j4)t}=e^{-1.5t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now the first factor, $e^{-1.5t}$, is the envelope, and the second factor, $e^{j4t}$, is the oscillatory term. That is the only split you need for this step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left|x(t)\\right|=e^{-1.5t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the envelope, read the decay rate as $1.5$ and note that the amplitude shrinks exponentially with $t$. From the oscillatory factor, read the angular frequency as $4$ rad/s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j4t}=\\cos(4t)+j\\sin(4t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $x(t)$ is a damped oscillation: it rotates at $4$ rad/s while its amplitude falls like $e^{-1.5t}$. The same split works whenever $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$ has a negative real part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Damped Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Decay Rate from Negative $\\sigma$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Work through $x(t)=e^{(-3+j2)t}$ by reading the decay rate from the negative real part and explaining how the envelope changes over time.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the negative real part in $-3+j2$. Split the exponent so the decaying envelope and the oscillation are separate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(-3+j2)t}=e^{-3t}e^{j2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $e^{-3t}$ is the envelope and $e^{j2t}$ is the oscillatory factor. The decay rate is the magnitude of the negative real part, so the amplitude decays at rate $3$ per unit time. The $j2$ term still gives angular frequency $2$ rad/s; it does not change the decay rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=e^{-3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\sigma=-3$, the amplitude shrinks toward zero as $t$ increases. A more negative $\\sigma$ would mean faster decay, while a less negative $\\sigma$ would mean slower decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Damped Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Angular Frequency from $\\omega$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Work through $x(t)=e^{(-1+j6)t}$ by isolating the oscillatory factor and reading the angular frequency from the imaginary part.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the imaginary part in $-1+j6$. Keep the damping factor fixed and read the oscillation from the $j6$ term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(-1+j6)t}=e^{-t}e^{j6t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $e^{-t}$ is the envelope and $e^{j6t}$ is the oscillatory factor. The angular frequency is the coefficient of $t$ in the imaginary exponent, so $\\omega=6$ rad/s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j6t}=\\cos(6t)+j\\sin(6t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Do not read frequency from the real part. The decay rate is $1$ per unit time, but the angular frequency is $6$ rad/s, so the signal oscillates at $6$ rad/s while its amplitude decays.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Damped Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Damped Sinusoid Behavior from $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Work through $x(t)=e^{(-2+j4)t}$ by interpreting the full damped-sinusoid behavior directly from $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the full complex parameter $s=-2+j4$. Read the real and imaginary parts together: the real part controls decay, and the imaginary part controls oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(-2+j4)t}=e^{-2t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\sigma=-2$ and $\\omega=4$. From $e^{-2t}$, the envelope decays at rate $2$ per unit time. From $e^{j4t}$, the angular frequency is $4$ rad/s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=e^{-2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal is a damped sinusoid: it oscillates at $4$ rad/s while its amplitude shrinks toward zero. The real part sets damping, and the imaginary part sets frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
